{"kind":"stack","id":"stk-dissolve","title":"Dissolving and recovering substances","theme":{"background":"#eef7ff","foreground":"#1d3557","font":"Arial, sans-serif"}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c1","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c1-t","content":"Dissolving"},{"type":"text","id":"c1-e","content":"When a solid mixes into a liquid and seems to disappear, it has dissolved. The mixture is called a solution."},{"type":"button","id":"c1-n","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c2","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c2-q","content":"Which of these will dissolve in water?"},{"type":"button","id":"c2-a","label":"Salt","why":"Salt dissolves in water to form a salt solution.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c2-b","label":"Sand","why":"Pupils may think all small grains mix in, but sand does not dissolve.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c2-c","label":"Pebbles","why":"Pupils may think hard materials always stay whole, but pebbles do not dissolve.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c3","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c3-q","content":"What do we call a mixture where a solid has dissolved in a liquid?"},{"type":"button","id":"c3-a","label":"A solution","why":"A solution is the clear mixture formed when a solid dissolves in a liquid.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-b","label":"A suspension","why":"Pupils may mix this up with solutions, but suspension particles settle out.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-c","label":"A gas","why":"Pupils may think any mixture is a gas, but a gas is a different state of matter.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c4","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c4-t","content":"Evaporation"},{"type":"text","id":"c4-e","content":"Evaporation is when a liquid turns into a gas. Heating a solution makes the liquid evaporate and leaves the solid behind."},{"type":"button","id":"c4-n","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c5","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c5-q","content":"Mia has salty water in a dish. She wants her salt back. What should she do?"},{"type":"button","id":"c5-a","label":"Leave it in the sun to dry","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c6"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c5-b","label":"Pour it down the sink","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c6","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c6-t","content":"The water slowly evaporates. Salt crystals are left in the dish. Well done, Mia!"},{"type":"button","id":"c6-n","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c7","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c7-t","content":"The salt goes down the sink with the water. Mia cannot get it back. Try again next time!"},{"type":"button","id":"c7-n","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c8","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c8-q","content":"Which method gets a dissolved solid back out of a solution?"},{"type":"button","id":"c8-a","label":"Evaporating the liquid","why":"Heating the solution evaporates the liquid and leaves the solid behind.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c8-b","label":"Adding more water","why":"Pupils may think more water helps, but this only dilutes the solution.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c8-c","label":"Shaking the jar","why":"Pupils may think shaking separates things, but shaking does not recover a dissolved solid.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c9","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c9-q","content":"Sugar is stirred into warm tea until it disappears. What has happened?"},{"type":"button","id":"c9-a","label":"The sugar has dissolved","why":"The sugar mixes fully into the tea and cannot be seen, so it has dissolved.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c9-b","label":"The sugar has melted","why":"Pupils may mix up melting and dissolving, but melting needs heat, not a liquid.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c9-c","label":"The sugar has evaporated","why":"Pupils may think everything that vanishes evaporates, but sugar stays in the tea.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c10","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c10-q","content":"Which of these will NOT dissolve in water?"},{"type":"button","id":"c10-a","label":"Sand","why":"Sand grains stay separate in water and do not dissolve.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c10-b","label":"Sugar","why":"Pupils may think sweet things always disappear, but sugar dissolves in water.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c10-c","label":"Salt","why":"Pupils may think all white powders dissolve, but salt dissolves in water.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c11","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c11-q","content":"After a solid dissolves, which statement is true?"},{"type":"button","id":"c11-a","label":"The solid is still in the liquid","why":"Dissolved solid is still present, just spread out so we cannot see it.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c12"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c11-b","label":"The solid has been destroyed","why":"Pupils may think it disappears completely, but the matter is still there.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c12"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c11-c","label":"The solid has turned into gas","why":"Pupils may think dissolving changes the solid into a gas, but it stays in the liquid.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c12"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c12","kind":"end","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c12-h","content":"Well done! You finished the dissolving and solutions stack."},{"type":"text","id":"c12-m","content":"You now know which materials dissolve, what a solution is, and how to recover a dissolved solid by evaporating the liquid."}],"actions":[]}}
